WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.180
How does a brain wire itself, partner by partner?

2
00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.300
Start with one small circuit.

3
00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:11.923
Tap the tendon below the knee, and the leg kicks.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:19.267
A sensory neuron in the thigh muscle excites the motor neurons of that same muscle.

5
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.170
Through an inhibitory interneuron, it also quiets the motor neurons of the opposing

6
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.170
muscle.

7
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:28.693
The right partners, with the right signs.

8
00:00:29.553 --> 00:00:31.733
Development built every one of those connections.

9
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:37.473
Now scale up. A human brain has about 86 billion neurons.

10
00:00:38.533 --> 00:00:41.733
They are joined by something like 100 trillion synapses.

11
00:00:42.867 --> 00:00:47.167
The genome that builds them has about 20,000 protein-coding genes.

12
00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.210
Could the genome simply list the connections?

13
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:56.083
Even using every letter of the genome, the list would be about a million times too

14
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.083
long.

15
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:00.127
So is the trouble giving every neuron its own name?

16
00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.500
No. Names are cheap.

17
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.420
Each extra on-off gene doubles the number of combinations.

18
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.400
A few dozen genes could give every neuron its own name.

19
00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.310
But a name says which cell you are.

20
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:25.510
It does not say whom to connect to.

21
00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.190
A label is not a wire.

22
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.240
So the genome stores rules.

23
00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.700
The first is a graded label.

24
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.740
Retinal ganglion cells carry a receptor whose amount depends on where they sit in

25
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:42.740
the eye.

26
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:47.143
Their target in the brain carries the matching cue, in an opposing gradient.

27
00:01:48.183 --> 00:01:49.943
The second is a local rule.

28
00:01:50.783 --> 00:01:54.523
Each growing tip, the growth cone, reads only what is right next to it.

29
00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:59.653
Axons with more receptor are kept away from places with more cue.

30
00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:07.187
In our toy model, each axon settles where receptor times cue reaches a set

31
00:02:07.187 --> 00:02:12.307
threshold. That rule and the opposing gradients give the right overall order.

32
00:02:13.327 --> 00:02:15.947
Real maps also use competition, and more cues.

33
00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:20.970
Now take 200 evenly spaced axons and a tenfold gradient.

34
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:25.930
Suppose each reading has an independent random error of about 10 percent.

35
00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:31.690
In this toy model, overall order stays high, but neighbouring pairs land in the

36
00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.510
right order only about 53 percent of the time.

37
00:02:37.483 --> 00:02:40.683
A third rule sharpens that: overproduce, then select.

38
00:02:41.717 --> 00:02:44.277
Each axon branches more widely than it needs.

39
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.320
Neighbouring cells in the retina tend to fire together.

40
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:54.023
A branch among its retinal neighbours fires in step with them, and together they

41
00:02:54.023 --> 00:02:55.543
drive the same target cells.

42
00:02:56.503 --> 00:02:57.783
Such branches tend to stay.

43
00:02:58.823 --> 00:03:00.703
Branches among strangers tend to retract.

44
00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:06.467
The tissue measures this after the connections form, so the genome never has to

45
00:03:06.467 --> 00:03:07.467
store it.

46
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.140
Here is a test. A goldfish can regrow a cut optic nerve.

47
00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:18.760
Block every spike from its eye with tetrodotoxin while the axons grow back.

48
00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:23.323
Which part of the map survives: the rough order, or the fine precision?

49
00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:33.087
The axons still reached their target, in roughly the right order.

50
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:38.387
But their branches stayed spread over a wide area, instead of clustering tightly.

51
00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.350
Molecular cues probably placed them.

52
00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.150
Activity sharpened them.

53
00:03:46.533 --> 00:03:50.653
Now block spikes in zebrafish embryos, as their first map forms.

54
00:03:51.473 --> 00:03:54.873
The map formed in order, and single arbors stayed small and tight.

55
00:03:55.883 --> 00:03:58.023
So the line can fall in different places.

56
00:03:58.963 --> 00:04:03.223
But these fish also differ in age, and in regrowing a map versus building one.

57
00:04:06.717 --> 00:04:10.897
The takeaway: the genome stores rules, and the tissue measures the rest.

58
00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:18.410
The course follows those rules in three acts: where cells go, how axons find their

59
00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.650
targets, and who survives.
